<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dominique de Gourgues</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1530–<a href="/years/1593">1593</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dominique de Gourgues was a French soldier from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. In 1568, to avenge the Spanish massacre of the Huguenot colonists of Fort Caroline in 1565, he led a privately financed expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, where, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timucua">Timucua</a> allies, he captured the Spanish forts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-river">St Johns River</a> and hanged their garrisons. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> without founding a settlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dominique-de-gourgues.jpg" alt="Dominique de Gourgues" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dominique_de_Gourgues.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Gourgues's early life. He came from the old and powerful Gourgue family, one of the most important of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>. He served in the Italian wars under Maréchal de Strozzi, was captured by the Spaniards in 1557 and then by the Turks, and spent several years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>. After his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he made a voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, then entered the service of the Duke de Guise and fought against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>. He was a captain in the army of King Charles IX.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1565 troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a> captured Fort Caroline, in what is now Jacksonville, killed nearly all their prisoners and hung the bodies on trees with the words Not as Frenchmen but as Lutherans; the Massacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas-inlet">Matanzas Inlet</a> followed. The killings angered Catholics and Protestants alike in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but Menéndez was rewarded rather than punished. Gourgues, embittered by what he himself had suffered at Spanish hands, resolved to avenge the Protestant colonists although he was a Catholic.</p><p>He sold everything he owned, borrowed from his brother Antoine, and chartered three ships with two hundred men, telling them his goal only once they reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, where they approved it. He then attacked the old Fort Caroline, renamed Fort San Mateo by the Spaniards, with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saturiwa">Saturiwa</a> and Tacatacuru clans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timucua">Timucua</a>, who had been the French fort's allies. The fort soon surrendered, and the French and their allies killed the Spanish prisoners, hanging them under the inscription Not as Spaniards but as murderers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gourgues landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> on 6 June 1568. Monluc, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, received him warmly, but the court, fearing a break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, was cold, and for several years he lived in obscurity and near poverty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>. Restored to royal favour in 1572, he commanded the largest vessel of the squadron at the Siege of La Rochelle. In 1592 Don Antonio de Crato made him commander of the fleet for a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> for the crown of Portugal, and he died on the voyage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dominique de Gourgues” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dominique de Gourgues</b> avenged them in 1568, but France did not return, leaving Spanish Florida in control.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-florida">French Florida</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mont-de-Marsan, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.89,-0.5&amp;year=1570" aria-label="Mont-de-Marsan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4538 609 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dominique de Gourgues"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4988 879h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4988 879h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.89,-0.5&amp;year=1570">What was here in 1570? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
